  "live_records": {
    "q-336528": {
      "dom_id": "step-q336528",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "A series RL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $8\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $40\\,Ω,$ and an inductance of $4\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ (in amperes) in the circuit at time $t$ (in seconds).",
      "worked_solution": "Kirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=40\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=40I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n8=40I+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+10I=2.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
