    "q-336534": {
      "dom_id": "step-q336534",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14146545/assets/c644931a5d7c3b015e00518269920718cde39882b45d3589e12e6b90d0391d46.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ (in amperes) at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.",
      "worked_solution": "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $9\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $36\\,Ω,$ and an inductor with an inductance of $3\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=36\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=36I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=3\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=3\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n9=36I+3\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+12I=3.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
