    "q-336549": {
      "dom_id": "step-q336549",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14146545/assets/a84a1501854f6efb988b7c02c3a1b5a80b80dbf3d5fc7ce94406778cc6dcadb5.png)\n\nFind the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ (in amperes) at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit given in the diagram above.",
      "worked_solution": "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $E(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $10\\,Ω,$ and an inductor with an inductance of $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=10\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=10I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)=10I+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+5I=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-336549-a",
